      "problem": "Let $a=-52$ and $n=9.$ Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a=qn+r,$ where $0\\le r<n,$ and determine $q\\cdot r.$",
      "worked_solution": "Dividing $-52$ by $9$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n-6<-52÷9<-5.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q=-6.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by solving $r=a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=-52-(-6)(9)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $-56=qn+r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-52={{(-6)}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 9+{{2}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $q\\cdot r=-6\\cdot 2=-12.$",
